From f61ac8e577aa2bf4c88d3b7f2b45d3ee7702edd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt C <latentcollapse@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:04:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 43/45] neurajl: the kernel's mid-call host requests reach the
 session's host handlers; Neura.rlm

A base-tools factory now receives its session's host handlers (lazily, the
same set the IPython kernel gets). NeuraJL answers the kernel's host_request
events with them, tagged with the requesting call's source as IPython's are,
so Julia's Neura.rlm reaches the existing RLM orchestration; there is no
second implementation. The permission is the kernel's ceiling
(host_request.allowed_types; NEURAJL_RLM_REQUEST_TYPES for RLM); the tool
description names Neura.rlm only when it is granted. RLM children get the
same factory, so the same ceiling.

Real-kernel tests: spawn+collect in one call and across calls, a failing
child reported exactly as the chassis reports it, host errors as Julia
errors, no permission means no child and no mention, and a child outliving
its kernel found again after revival. Red control: without the dispatch the
spawn times out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
